1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:01,815
Why does mint feel cold?

2
00:00:02,115 --> 00:00:04,545
The mint isn't cooling your mouth down.

3
00:00:05,045 --> 00:00:11,493
Nerves in your mouth carry a tiny cold sensor: a gate that opens when the temperature drops.

4
00:00:11,993 --> 00:00:16,408
Menthol, the molecule in mint, latches onto that same gate.

5
00:00:16,708 --> 00:00:21,011
Now it opens at warmer temperatures, even in a warm mouth.

6
00:00:21,611 --> 00:00:25,422
The nerve fires, and your brain gets one message: cold.

7
00:00:25,922 --> 00:00:29,721
That's why a sip of water after a mint can feel icy.

8
00:00:29,721 --> 00:00:31,839
The sensor is already primed.

9
00:00:32,339 --> 00:00:35,763
Chili plays the same trick on your burning-heat sensor.

10
00:00:36,263 --> 00:00:37,585
Mint isn't cold.

11
00:00:37,585 --> 00:00:39,980
It just rings the cold alarm.
